I don't know about all juristictions, but it is my understanding
(from time spent in law-enforcement many years ago) that it is a
felony in California to possess burglary tools WITH FELONIOUS
INTENT.  There is no specific definition of a "burglary tool".
Lockpicks are perfectly legal if you do not intend to commit
crimes.  In fact, there is a store near where I work in San
Francisco that sells picks, pick guns, etc. to the public.  I
think it's called the Spy Shop and is a nationwide chain.  A
hammer and a crowbar are likewise legal when used to remodel a
house but a felony to possess if breaking into a store.  Some of
these legal issues are discussed in "The MIT Guide To
Lockpicking"  (if I remember the title correctly) which was a
student paper describing how locks work, how to pick them, and
how to grind your own picks from street-sweeper bristles.

Interestingly, you don't have to *use* the tools to make their
possession illegal.  All that must be proved is possession and
intent.  If you find the back door unlocked and don't need the
crowbar you have still shown intent.  The same could be claimed
if you are busted while planning a break-in with your pals.

Does this mean that possession of a modem is a felony if you intend
to steal data??  Beats me - I'm not a laywer.

