I don't think this is uncommon.  We have had problems with users in
the past, and on occasion they raised such a stink with the school
administration that we were told to give them what they want.  It was
made quite clear that the administration was not interested in the
issues, but in reducing the noise level.  We were not dealing with
serious cracking here (in most cases), but with management issues such
as games (which were slugging the system) and space allocations.  

We also are dealing with a situation where a faculty member has apparently
(allegedly) given out his personal account to a particularly troublesome
former student.  Although policy clearly states accounts will not be
shared, we don't expect much upper-level support dealing with this
problem, either.  The situation is complicated by the computing area
reporting to the Finance VP, not to anyone on the academic side.

We also have problems with faculty members who say things like "get those
games off the system; I'm trying to run a lab," and then do something like
give a student academic credit for writing a game manual (which, of course,
requires that the game run for a couple of quarters 'to gather data'."


I'm bothered by the commonly expressed idea that "Anything that isn't
illegal is permissable."  We've apparently turned over what should be
social issues, such as ethical issues, to the judicial and law
enforcement establishments.  What about the idea that the community
has the right to set behavioral standards for its members?  Rude behavior
may not be illegal, but that doesn't mean it has to be tolerated. Does
the community have to accept behavior on their computer systems that would
not be acceptable in face-to-face interactions?  Are we forced to 
resort to draconian measures such as total banishment from our systems
or involvement of law enforcement to deal with disruptive individuals
simply because there are no other tools available.

I would look for more effective tools for individual accountability and
a finer-grained approach to asset control.  Since the problem users are
the minority, perhaps some tools that work in reverse would be useful,
such as exclusion groups or reverse acl's that could be used as a 
selective "time-out box" for specific resources, without affecting
significantly the individuals ability to accomplish academic work.

--All ramblings are my personal opinions.

