From: SBVEEDER@SUVM.BITNET (Stacy B Veeder)
Subject:  Ethics and the Internet
Date: 21 Jun 1993 18:57:01 -0400
Message-ID: <199306212256.AA28175@eff.org>

Okay, here's my two cents...  I think we can all agree, first off, that if
the professor HAD told the students that their correspondence was a part of
a research study (particularly if the students had the option not to
participate), there would be no violation of law, ethics, human-subjects
research codes, etc.  But human-subjects research sometimes requires that
the subjects NOT know that they are being observed, lest the fact of
observation itself biases the results.  (There is lots of literature on
this phenomenon.)

So-  assuming, as the author of the original post on this subject stipulated,
that the students did NOT know...  If quotes are used without attribution,
particularly in such a way as to protect the identity of the author (i.e.,
not only doesn't the professor identify the author of the quote, but also
the quote itself contains nothing identifying the author), then you would
have a hard time prosecuting an invasion of privacy case based on the
published research.  Depending on the computing policies of the institution
in question, the students may or may not have a case for invasion of privacy
based on the professor's access to the correspondence in the first place.
(Whoever posted the initial scenario to which we're all responding did not
reveal how this access was accomplished...)

As for copyright-  as long as any quotes are short and attributed to "a
participant in this study," it's a quote, not an infringement.  Someone
has already summarized the fair-use argument, so I'll not repeat it here.
(Obviously, longer quotes or plagiarism are another matter...but one would
hope that neither would occur in a scholarly piece of research.)

If no quotes at all are used, i.e., if all of the content is aggregated
into statistics or into qualitative descriptions, then again the only
possible question of impropriety is how the professor gained access to
the original correspondence.  If the students gave the professor their
passwords (tsk tsk!!) or handed in printouts, it's hard to see that they
expected privacy...  If, on the other hand, the professor gained access
through a third party, say the sysops, without the students' knowledge,
then the students may want to explore the college's computing policies to
see whether this action was permitted by the administration.

For the record-  professors have always used student work to advance their
own research.  The only thing here that's new is the fact that the student
work is in the form of correspondence...and, as someone here has pointed
out, it is public correspondence.

So-  one more opinion to stir the stew...   :)

