>A class discussion list or conference is a requirement placed upon you.  Your
>grade is likely based in part upon your participation.  Use of your 'emails'
>in this sense should only be applied to the purpose stated in that
requirement.
You're right; this is an important distinction that I missed.  I agree that
if participation is required for the class, and students therefore might not
participate otherwise, it is inappropriate for the posts to be used by the
instructor for any other purpose.  (Note, however, that if the professor
requires his students to participate on, say, a Usenet or Listserv group,
there is no way for *other* researchers to reasonably infer that there's
anything special about those posts, unless the students put something in
their .sig files.  To anyone else, they look like ordinary posts, which
I still maintain are suitable for use by researchers.)

>If other uses are intended by the listowner (prof) that should be known up
>front to allow the student to participate as they feel appropriate once having
>been informed.

As someone else pointed out, some (most? all?) psychological and sociological
research is blown if the subjects know what's going on.  I would find it
acceptable for the professor to collect his data without students knowing,
but at the end of the class tell them and then delete the data from any
student who objects.  He'd then be free to use the rest.  Whether it would
be statistically valid to do so would be a determination the professor would
have to make himself.

