Putting Out the Fires

By Paul Evan Peters

     It is difficult to anticipate all of the things that people, 
including members of your campus community, will say and do on the 
network. This is one lesson that we've learned so far in the networked 
environment. What's more, it's impossible to predict how others, within 
or outside your campus or community, will react to various postings or 
behaviors on the net. We need to be better prepared to deal with 
surprises of both types.
     Because it's not always possible to avoid a crisis, what do you do 
when one arises and a media spotlight is glaring on your institution? 
What do you tell the media, for example, if your institution supports a 
connection to your local K-12 school system and you learn that one of 
your students is posting obscenities that can be accessed by second 
graders? 
     Or, how do you respond to the press when a student at your 
institution has sent through the network a virus that is shutting down 
computers across the country?
     You need to be prepared. The Coalition for Networked Information 
(CNI) Working Group on Legislation, Codes, Policies, and Practices has 
been charged with working on these kinds of issues. Under the leadership 
of Peter Graham, associate university librarian for Technology and 
Networked Information Services at Rutgers University, and Richard 
Akeroyd Jr., state librarian at Connecticut State Library, this group is 
targeting its efforts on, among other things, developing strategies to 
help you prevent these types of situations from spinning out of control.
     The group plans to focus on products that will help you prepare for 
such situations by using a draft press kit that suggests what to do when 
a local problem arises and the press is immediately on the scene, as 
well as by establishing a model policy for appropriate use of the 
network. In addition, the group plans to provide guidelines on such 
issues as confidentiality, privacy, pseudonymity, anonymity, free 
speech, and responsible use.
     Thus far the group has issued Information Policies: A Compilation 
of Position Statements, Principles, Statutes, and Other Pertinent 
Statements, which brings together the original texts of official 
statements of organizations and associations, principles, and laws 
related to information policy. The document is available in the 
coalition's ftp archive at ftp.cni.org.
     To join a discussion on social, legal, and ethical issues in the 
networked information environment, subscribe to the coalition electronic 
forum cni-legis by sending an e-mail message to LISTSERV@CNI.ORG. The 
message should read: subscribe <your name> cni-legis. You may also 
contact the Working Group leaders directly at 
rakeroyd@eagle.wesleyan.edu or graham@ zodiac.rutgers.edu.

