initiative in the area of accreditation guidelines and practices.
     Pacific Neighborhood Project. The first meeting of member 
institutions of the Pacific Neighborhood Project, formed to support 
improved information exchange between Pacific Rim countries, was held 
January 14 and 15, 1993, in Honolulu. The goal of the project is to 
facilitate scholarly exchanges of information through shared use of 
computing and communications technology, thereby creating a virtual 
community that minimizes the great distances between many institutions 
in this region. The meeting drew representatives from Australia, Canada, 
Fiji, Hong Kong, Japan, Korea, New Zealand, People's Republic of China, 
Republic of China, Singapore, Thailand, and the United States and 
addressed membership conditions, provision of services and benefits, 
program areas, and future meetings. 
     In a prepared address to participants, Clifford Lynch, head of 
library systems, University of California Office of the President, and 
leader of the coalition's Working Group on Architectures, described the 
partnership model of the CNI and the way it has facilitated 
collaborative relationships among librarians, technologists, publishers, 
and others in the networking community. Curtis Hardyck, chair of the 
Pacific Neighborhood Consortium Steering Committee, suggested that the 
coalition serve as an organizational model for this newly forming group. 
     The Pacific Neighborhood Project was initiated at the University of 
California at Berkeley, with the assistance and support of the Asia 
