The Community of Electronic Learners

By Frank W. Connolly and Chuck Schneebeck

     Communities generally develop in close geographic proximity, 
limited by time and space. Each of us is born into a community that is 
strange to us. Over time, we develop an awareness of our community and 
learn the norms of behavior and the rules of conduct appropriate for it. 
The advent of the automobile, train, airplane, and telephone eliminated 
some of the barriers of time and space, enabling all of us to join other 
communities.
     As we mature, we broaden ourselves by becoming members of other 
communities: for instance, we become a Republican or a Democrat, we 
change locales, or we join a church. However, we are not an effective 
member of a group and its community if we are never involved with its 
meetings or activities. Being a member requires participation.
     The formation of communities of electronic learners is facilitated 
by computer networks. Electronic communities are composed of individuals 
with common interests who are linked by computers over communications 
networks. These networks enable collaboration between learners by 
removing time and distance barriers and providing faculty and students 
with access to information that is not otherwise available.
     This ability to reach beyond the traditional boundaries of 
educational institutions in order to interact with others and to access 
information is changing the size and composition of the educational 
community. For example, schoolchildren and scholars communicate across 
thousands of miles to pose questions, solicit opinions, and learn 
answers. Networks enable individuals to reach out to and join learning 
communities without local organizational approval, support, or control.
     Electronic communities have great potential for overcoming the 
barriers of race, age, and appearance. All of the cues used in face-to-
face communication to establish rapport or barriers are removed because 
the characters and images on a computer screen are the same, regardless 
of who creates them. Therefore, electronic communities link individuals 
who may never meet face-to-face. 

New Challenges
     Electronic communities evolve rapidly, previously a rare 
occurrence. Historically, communities develop slowly, allowing 
individuals to assimilate the values and ethics of the community. Both 
the community and its members mature and evolve over time. Rapid 
evolution and its associated changes make that process more difficult. 
Without the opportunity to evaluate and interpret visible clues and to 
experience firsthand the behavior and reactions of others, electronic 
communities sometimes have difficulty establishing and propagating the 
norms for behavior. It becomes difficult to answer questions such as:
     * Are new members expected to jump right into an ongoing 
discussion?
     * Should new members hold back and wait until asked to join in 
discussions?
     * In responding to another's stated opinion, should we ask 
questions and hope to draw out a point, or should we state our opinions 
forcefully?
     * Do we inquire about the personal aspects of others, or should we 
stay strictly on an impersonal level?
     The community of electronic learners is evolving as a subset of the 
greater academic community, operating under a shared set of norms of 
behavior and rules of conduct. Yet many questions remain about how 
traditional values will be applied in this new setting. Time and 
experience will help us in answering many of these questions. It seems 
obvious, though, that the context of communities and the objectives for 
which they are intended will greatly influence how electronic 
communities operate.

Electronic Communities and Rights
     The Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners is 
intended to facilitate the establishment and functioning of the new 
electronic communities by building upon the underlying values and 
objectives of education. It highlights rights and responsibilities for 
individuals as well as organizations in the community.
     These rights include access to computer resources and the training 
necessary to use the resources effectively, control over the collection 
and distribution of personal information, freedom of speech, and the 
right to exercise ownership over intellectual works created in 
electronic communities.

Electronic Communities and Responsibilities
     Citizenship in a community brings with it responsibilities as well. 
For any community to remain dynamic and effective, its citizenry must 
take an active role. The Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for 
Electronic Learners proposes a set of responsibilities that each member 
of the electronic community needs to respect and actuate if the 
community is to be vibrant and effective.
     These responsibilities include actively pursuing training and 
resources rather than waiting for others to create or deliver them, 
respecting the intellectual works of others, maintaining awareness of 
the nature and importance of information and the need to ensure that it 
is accurate, acting ethically and respecting the rights and values of 
other members of the community, and recognizing and respecting that the 
community of learners shares valuable and limited resources.

Fostering the Electronic Community
     If these responsibilities are not respected and fulfilled, then 
either the citizens of the electronic community of learners will be 
forced to expend their energies in building individual defenses to 
protect themselves, or they will decide not to effectively utilize the 
opportunities that electronic communities afford learners.
     Failure to participate actively in the electronic community of 
learners will be a loss to the individual and to the community as a 
whole. Cut off from the benefits of accessing a world of resources, the 
individual becomes less well educated and less well prepared to compete 
in an electronic world. The electronic community will thus lose the 
knowledge, experience, and insights of those individuals. But if every 
citizen in the electronic community recognizes and acts to fulfill the 
responsibilities, he or she can participate fully because the community 
will be built on trust and shared values. The electronic community of 
learners and the individuals will all grow and benefit.




