New Policies for an Evolving Future

By Frank W. Connolly and Sally Webster

     Technology is dynamic. By the time you acquire and learn to use a 
piece of technology--whether software or hardware--it is probably at 
least updated and is often outdated. Before we mastered DOS 4.0, DOS 5.0 
was out, along with OS/2 and Windows. Before we learned to exploit the 
power of stand-alone systems, local-area networks and wide-area networks 
were common. Each advance is accompanied by new potential and new ways 
of doing things.
     Even as we are frustrated by technology's dynamic nature, education 
is growing increasingly dependent on it. Computers and computing are no 
longer frills to be added to one's educational experience. Rather, they 
are important components that are quickly becoming crucial. Even so, we 
must guard against letting technology change the underlying values of 
education. To ensure that those values are not lost, we as educators 
need to recognize that although the context for learning may be 
changing, the underlying values ought not change. We must develop and 
advocate policies specifically to protect those underlying values.
     The Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners 
constitutes an attempt to address this dynamic facet of education. It is 
offered for review and consideration, not as a fait accompli policy 
statement to be adopted without change. Rather, it is a model that 
educators can use to develop policies and procedures that will protect 
the values we consider important while it improves learning, teaching, 
and research through the use of technology. It proposes an ideal--
something we in the dynamic environment of education strive toward. It 
is not intended to address today's problems; nor is it intended for 
immediate implementation. Rather, the Bill of Rights and 
Responsibilities for Electronic Learners proposes policy for our 
evolving future.

Education's Values Extend to the Electronic Community
     The electronic community of learners is a subset of the entire 
educational community. It includes those who would use technology as a 
part of their teaching, learning, and research. As members of the 
educational community, the electronic communities of learners espouse 
the values that are supported in education. These values developed and 
evolved over generations and encompass such areas as academic freedom, 
equality, respect for diverse opinions and for the law, and commitment 
to ethical and responsible behavior.
     The introduction of technology does not change these values but 
will affect how education is accomplished. For instance, technology may 
radically alter how course content is presented or it may offer new 
learning experiences and education that is tailored to individuals. It 
may enable students and teachers to share ideas and insights across the 
boundaries of time and space. However, technology does not change 
underlying values any more than a new school building changes the values 
and talents of the students and teachers who use it.

Rights and Responsibilities
     Education in the United States is characterized by diversity. 
School systems are under the control of local school boards, which 
reflect the diversity of their community, rather than the United States 
Department of Education. On the postsecondary level, the United States 
is revered worldwide for the diversity of its institutions of higher 
education.
     Given such diversity, it is impossible to define with specificity a 
policy that is appropriate for all. For this reason, the Bill of Rights 
and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners offers broad statements of 
general principles rather than specific and detailed rules.
     For example, the bill proposes that access to computing is a right 
for all students. This does not mean that every school or college is 
expected to provide a computer for every student any more than the 
Second Amendment to the U.S. Constitution (the right to bear arms) 
requires the federal government to supply weapons to every citizen. 
Rather, we offer a broad principle that reflects the increasing 
importance of technology in education. In accepting that principle and 
implementing it through specific policies and rules, an organization 
must address the role that technology plays in its mission.
     Today, the policy statements of many districts and institutions 
treat computing as a privilege so as to more easily enforce rules 
surrounding its use or abuse by the threat of revoking the privilege. 
Think of a student misusing a chalkboard. Would we accept an arbitrary 
punishment of banning the student from classrooms containing 
chalkboards? As the computer becomes integral to learning, we should 
have similar concern about removing access to it.
     Each of the articles in the Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for 
Electronic Learners offers similar material for consideration, 
discussion, and interpretation as each institution or district strives 
to move toward the ideal proposed in the bill. Because of the diversity 
of institutions, this movement will take place school by school and 
campus by campus, rather than as a sweeping national movement.

A Guide for Policy Development
     The goal is to offer a document that educational organizations can 
use as a guide and model in developing their own policies regarding 
computers and technology. We do not expect necessarily that any district 
or institution will adopt verbatim the Bill of Rights and 
Responsibilities for Electronic Learners. Rather, we intend it to 
indicate topics for consideration, identify potential policy issues, and 
propose ideas and ideals for addressing issues. The benefit of the bill 
is in fostering consideration and discussion of those issues at all 
levels of education.
     It is hoped that in addition to fostering policy and discussion 
within the educational community, the Bill of Rights and 
Responsibilities for Electronic Learners will stimulate other 
communities to develop similar statements. As technology becomes more 
prevalent in education, so too does it invade government and business. 
Just as in education, broad policy issues in other sectors need to be 
addressed.

The Bill--Now and Future
     The material presented in this issue of EDUCOM Review reflects work 
in progress. Articles contained herein address and explain some of the 
critical issues that the Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for 
Electronic Learners touches upon: computing as a right or a privilege, 
the meaning of an electronic community and its citizens, and the culture 
of learning and the role of technology. These are offered to provide the 
reader with a richer understanding of the issues and to explain more 
fully how we came to the positions we did in drafting the bill.
     The Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners 
will likely remain in draft form through October 1993. During this year, 
we hope to present and discuss the bill in many educational forums and 
to receive responses from many quarters. Following EDUCOM'93, we will 
finalize the wording of the bill and offer it for wide distribution and 
acceptance. Periodically we expect to revisit the bill both to ensure 
that it reflects the model toward which we should be striving and to 
investigate the effects it has had on teaching and learning.
     On another level, this bill constitutes the initial phase of a 
broader project. We are already moving to coordinate similar bills of 
rights and responsibilities for government, business, and technology. 
The Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners will be 
used as a model by those other communities and will, it is hoped, lead 
to the development of subsequent bills of rights and responsibilities 
for electronic citizens. It is hoped that a single Bill of Rights and 
Responsibilities for Electronic Communities will eventually emerge from 
these sectors, offering guidelines for all organizations and individuals 
involved in electronic communities.
     This process is expected to take several years. We hope that every 
phase of the project will act as a catalyst for discussion of critical 
issues, fostering consideration of the rights and responsibilities of 
individuals and organizations.






