Privacy in the Electronic Community

By Terrie Spetalnick 

The electronic community brings with it the potential for massive new 
threats to privacy. These threats are significant because of the 
extraordinary power of the technologies and because the concept of 
privacy in this area is ill defined.
     Privacy is a significant value for Americans. This is 
understandable given the potential consequences of losing privacy and 
having information fall into the wrong hands. Losing privacy could 
result in harassment, use of information for unauthorized purposes, 
endangerment of political expression, and so on. Worse yet, loss of 
privacy means loss of autonomy and personal liberty. The right to 
control knowledge about ourselves gives control over the context in 
which we act, allows us to define our relations with others, and 
ultimately allows each of us to define ourself.
     Like other ethical concerns, issues of privacy in the electronic 
arena are blurred by the complexity inherent in information technology 
and the differences between digital products and traditional forms of 
communication such as print media.

Digital Versus Print
Digital products are ephemeral compared to print documents, which are 
tangible and fixed in space and time. Digital information, more flexible 
than print documents, is more easily altered and more vulnerable to 
interception. Whereas printed documents are narrowly focused, millions 
of individuals can communicate more or less simultaneously with one 
another using computer networks.
     Nothing in the present legal or regulatory framework anticipates 
such a dispersion of information sources. Differing characteristics of 
medium make it difficult to consider digital productions as property and 
thus apply existing laws and regulations. Still, discussion within the 
electronic community about topics such as privacy has been couched 
predominantly in legal, economic, and technological frameworks.

Rights and Responsibilities in the Electronic Community
The Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners is an 
attempt to place the focus on values related to technology and the 
accompanying fundamental rights. Two of the individual rights enumerated 
by the bill deal with potential affronts to privacy:
     * the rights of electronic citizens with regard to personal 
information stored electronically and
     * the rights related to freedom of electronic speech.
     Despite the myriad powerful capabilities of computers, public 
debate on the privacy issue has centered on one of the computer's most 
primitive functions: its capacity to store, organize, and exchange 
records.

Electronically Stored Information
The bill states in Article I, Section 3, that citizens of the electronic 
community have the right to know that personal information about them is 
stored electronically. Citizens also have the right to review and 
correct that information and to control its distribution beyond the 
expressed purpose of its collection.
     Concern arises because of the possibilities created when 
information is stored in electronic form. The computer extends almost 
infinitely over paper medium the amount of information that can be 
stored, and it increases processing and communication capabilities by 
orders of magnitude. The exchange of large amounts of information is 
made incredibly quick and easy. Exchange can take place with or without 
the knowledge of the person whom the information is about, and it can 
take place intentionally as well as unintentionally.
     Enormous storage capabilities obviate the need to purge old 
information, so records might easily be kept for a person's lifetime. 
Because of great facility of exchange, the effect of a small error can 
be magnified enormously. These conditions create the possibility that 
incidents in one's life or errors in accompanying records could 
profoundly affect how one is perceived and treated. Personal records are 
likely to affect decisions by a wide variety of agencies, from employers 
to medical care providers to law enforcement agencies.
     Americans have some ambivalence about relinquishing privacy when 
interacting with strangers. On the one hand, we believe that privacy is 
a fundamental right and are concerned about threats to personal privacy. 
On the other, we acknowledge that organizations need information about 
individuals in order to make decisions.

Institutional Versus Individual Ownership
Questions regarding access to higher education institutional data have 
in some part been answered by legal precedents, such as the so-called 
Buckley Amendment (Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act of 1974). 
This amendment grants students access to personal information, such as 
transcripts and disciplinary reports, created by their institution. 
Consistent with the corporate view that the company owns the data on its 
employees, two thirds of computer center directors surveyed at the 
SIGUCCS '92 management symposium agreed that a university owns staff 
files stored on university-owned computer equipment. The same group was 
split over who owns faculty and student files.1
     Exercise of individual and institutional responsibility is clearly 
necessary for adequate protection of the privacy of individuals with 
regard to electronically stored personal data. A computer professional 
asked for data without legal guidance can make choices about the 
ownership of it by either:
     1. assuming the information is in the public realm until there is 
some evidence that it is not or
     2. assuming the information is private unless or until there is 
evidence that it is not.
     Given the importance of privacy in the American value system, it 
would seem that professionals should always adopt the second rule.
     The bill espouses such a standard for institutional responsibility 
in Article IV, Section 3. This article calls for institutions to treat 
electronically stored personal files as confidential, examining or 
disclosing the contents only when authorized by the information's owner 
or an appropriate institutional official or when required by local, 
state, or federal law.
     The bill also advocates in Article II, Section 3, individual 
responsibility with regard to personal data. Each citizen is urged to 
take responsibility for the integrity of all information, to be aware of 
the possible effects of manipulating electronic information, and to 
verify the integrity and completeness of information that he or she 
compiles or uses.

Freedom of Speech and Privacy
Another growing dilemma in electronic communities is balancing the right 
of free speech against the right of privacy. The bill recognizes that 
citizens of electronic communities have the same constitutional right to 
freedom of speech as citizens of other communities (Article I, Sec. 4), 
but the use of computer networks, particularly in higher education, is 
growing far faster than is consensus on the rules governing privacy and 
free expression.
     This potential for abuse of free expression has manifested itself 
in many universities. Computer center directors have received complaints 
that the content of electronic bulletin boards or electronic mail 
messages has been harassing or obscene. Campus officials may be called 
upon to prevent disruption of the educational process or to prevent 
placing persons or property in danger by regulating the time, place, and 
manner of expression on electronic media. Yet they may have to make 
these decisions in the absence of general agreement about what the 
campus community considers decent and appropriate.
     The crux of the problem is determining whether such incidents 
merely constitute electronic free speech or whether they are 
transgressions on privacy. The question may best be answered by 
instituting policies that spell out prohibited conduct. Ideally, all 
segments of the campus should work together to clarify community values 
and impose upon themselves associated policies. In doing so, individuals 
acknowledge their responsibilities to the group and agree to buy in to 
the policies.

Responsible Behavior
In Article II, Section 4, the bill echoes the need for individual 
discipline, asserting that each citizen of the electronic community of 
learners must respect and value the privacy rights of all other citizens 
in that community and must behave ethically toward others. Article IV, 
Section 4, advocates an institutional role in shaping ethical behavior 
by helping faculty, staff, and students to understand ethical and legal 
uses of electronic resources.
     Although clear policies will go a long way toward preventing abuses 
of privacy, the key seems to lie in everyone's thinking through the 
issues. Unethical acts are easily obscured by the medium; computer 
abuses are considered somehow different from other abuses. But the 
ethics and values that tell us that libel is wrong should apply in the 
same way to its high-tech equivalent.
     It has been said that electronic communications are about as 
private as a postcard. Even if this is an exaggeration, it seems 
unlikely that policies and safeguards alone will guarantee privacy. The 
best hope seems to lie in efforts, such as the bill, to encourage 
members of the electronic community to define and adopt for themselves 
standards of behavior that acknowledge individual and institutional 
responsibilities as well as rights.

Endnote
     1. Stager, Susan F. "Computer ethics violations: More questions 
than answers." EDUCOM Review 27:(4) 29, 1992.

