Individual Rights Versus Institutional Responsibilities

By Susan Stager

The Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners proposes 
an ideal that educational institutions can strive to implement, 
reflecting their individual traditions and missions. Like the original 
Bill of Rights, the Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic 
Learners faces stiff debate. This draft bill reminds us that rights are 
accompanied by responsibilities. Individual rights and responsibilities 
are juxtaposed with and shaped by institutional rights and 
responsibilities, conflicting at times. Conflict between rights and 
responsibilities is inevitable. Debate about rights and responsibilities 
is welcome. Much of the debate centers on the issue of whether 
traditional categories of rights, such as privacy, freedom of speech, 
and due process, have application in the age of information technology 
and whether new ways of interpreting those older categories of rights 
can accommodate the technology. Debate about application of the Bill of 
Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners to the educational 
arena hinges on two questions epitomizing the conflict: Is the scope of 
individual faculty and student freedoms unlimited? If not, where do 
educational institutions draw the limits? For example: 
     * The Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners 
guarantees freedom of speech, but can an educational institution allow 
students to post messages that some groups perceive as intimidating? 
     * Does the Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic 
Learners include the right of individuals to post a confidential 
document on electronic bulletin boards? 
     The inherent conflict between individual rights and institutional 
responsibilities is one of many themes in the Bill of Rights and 
Responsibilities for Electronic Learners. The following sections attempt 
both to draw analogies between the original Bill of Rights and the Bill 
of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners and to identify 
potential conflicts between individual rights and institutions' 
responsibilities, which are posed as questions that need to be addressed 
as the bill is implemented.

Freedom of Expression
Freedom of speech is addressed in Article I, Section 4, of the Bill of 
Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners (see page 26). If 
electronic networks are to function as communication vehicles in the 
educational community, then faculty and students must be able to engage 
in the free exchange of ideas. The First Amendment of the U.S. 
Constitution holds an honored place among our basic rights, guaranteeing 
freedom of expression. As recently as 1992, the courts ruled that First 
Amendment rights have some limits. The meaning of these limits to 
private and public educational institutions is unclear. And due to that 
lack of clarity, educational institutions are struggling to word their 
network use policies so as to accommodate First Amendment rights while 
upholding their responsibilities to maintain a nonintimidating
environment. In addition, public educational institutions must uphold 
Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Civil Service Reform 
Act of 1978. Various constituencies in the educational community have 
questioned the appropriateness of some network activity, especially the 
"alt" groups, in light of this legislation. Thus, the individual right 
of free expression on the network may be in conflict with the 
institutional responsibility to uphold certain legislation. 

Freedom of the Press
Today electronic networks keep citizens informed, just as newspapers, 
pamphlets, and books did in the time of the writers of the constitution. 
One question that arose when the original Bill of Rights was written and 
that is relevant today is how far can the press go in criticizing the 
government? The analogous question is how far can postings on the 
electronic network go in criticizing the administration of an 
educational institution? There is also the issue of defamation liability 
of computer bulletin board operators and their educational institution. 
Operators of computer bulletin boards may be liable for defamation, 
copyright infringement, and violation of criminal statutes that create 
civil causes of action.1 What is libelous in the electronic community? 
Posting an untrue statement on a computer bulletin board about an 
institutional administrator is not necessarily libel, following the 
precedent set for the press. Computer bulletin boards are not 
specifically mentioned in the draft bill. Perhaps the more pressing 
issue is whether computer bulletin boards are analogous to a newspaper, 
book, or letter. Who owns the content and who has responsibility for the 
content? Is freedom of the press or freedom of expression the issue? 

Freedom of Assembly
Citizens have a right to assemble and to express their views. What is 
the parallel in the electronic community? Several visionaries have 
spoken of "common space" on the network. Will such common space be the 
site of assembly and
protest for faculty and students? The protest demonstration has a long 
history in the United States and in the educational community. When does 
the right of civil rights groups, antiwar groups, antiadministration 
groups, and many others in the educational community clash with the 
responsibilities of the institution providing access to the electronic 
network? For example, a major problem with demonstrations has always 
been that they sometimes interfere with other
people and their activities. If network resources are limited, 
protesters who are using that network may be denying network access to 
others. Or the cost of providing access for protesters may result in 
limited funds for other information technology resources. Can rules be 
made to prevent such inconveniences on the electronic network? Would 
such rules violate people's freedom of assembly? The bill addresses 
these issues in Article IV, Section 1 (see page 27).

Right to Privacy
The right of privacy for individual users of the electronic environment 
creates security responsibilities for the educational institution 
providing that electronic environment. This responsibility is addressed 
in the bill in Article IV, Section 3 (see page 27). Privacy is not 
specifically mentioned in the U.S. Constitution. A doctrine of the right 
to privacy has been deduced from the search-and-seizure clause and 
various parts of the First, Fourth, Fifth, Ninth, and Fourteenth 
amendments.
     The history of the courts' recognition of the right to privacy and 
of the possible threat posed by large-scale information collection and 
processing techniques is detailed by Murdock (1989).2 The use of 
computers and electronic networks by banks, credit agencies, and private 
businesses as well as by educational institutions has raised a whole set 
of questions about privacy that weren't issues until modern times.
     Not only does the electronic environment offer the possibility of 
institutional surveillance of individuals on a scale that would dwarf 
that which has gone before, but
there is also the possibility that individuals will find ways to access 
the network and use the confidential information stored on it. Where is 
the line between individual rights to privacy and institutional 
responsibilities for maintaining security?

Freedom From Unreasonable Searches
The Fourth Amendment guarantees "the right of the people to be secure in 
their persons,
houses, papers, and effects, against unreasonable searches and 
seizures." The Fourth Amendment does not prohibit all searches--only 
"unreasonable" ones. Many electronic citizens equate their electronic 
mailboxes and files with houses and papers, respectively. If the analogy 
is accepted, then some individual rights and institutional 
responsibilities are implied. What constitutes a reasonable search of 
electronic mailboxes and electronic files by educational institutions? 
It would appear that institutional administrators cannot just guess that 
they will find something in electronic mailboxes or electronic files. 
They must be able to say exactly what information they are looking for 
and why they will find it in a certain location. The conflict becomes 
apparent when educational institutions must maintain the integrity of 
the network. What onslaughts against the network justify a search of 
computer files or mailboxes without the equivalent of a warrant?
     What is a reasonable search in this circumstance? What is the role 
of computer matching in maintaining systems security?3

Due Process
The Fifth Amendment states that no person shall "be deprived of life, 
liberty, or property, without due process of law." The Fifth Amendment 
is relevant for the draft bill to the extent to which technology and 
access to technology are viewed as property. Many in the educational 
community view network access and file space as valuable property, if 
not a right. Article I, Section 1 (see page 26), of the draft bill 
boldly states that "A citizen's access to computing and information 
resources is a right. Access to computing or
information resources shall not be denied or removed without just 
cause." Can an institution deprive electronic citizens of access to the 
network and its resources without due process? Under what conditions? 
What responsibilities do educational institutions have to ensure that 
network resources not be denied without due process? Is there a 
difference between due process at private and due process at public 
educational institutions? When do responsibilities to maintain a secure
network environment outweigh due process rights of faculty or students?

Future Issues
Many sections of the original Bill of Rights appear to have relevance 
for the electronic environment today. In the future, even more sections 
of the original Bill of Rights may have relevance. The need to expand 
and amend the Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic 
Learners will arise as both societal values and technology change. It 
will be important for leadership to recognize the historical precedents 
of these conflicts and to exercise the political skills required for 
resolving them.

The Bill--Continued Debate
The draft bill attempts to articulate both the values that technology 
can facilitate and the accompanying fundamental rights. The draft bill 
also acknowledges that these rights are not absolute but come with 
related responsibilities.
     The inherent conflict between individual rights and institutional 
responsibilities is a central theme of the bill. Much as the framers of 
the constitution thought that a bill of rights was unnecessary because 
the new national government was going to have limited powers, some 
educators who are activists in the development of the national network 
are of the opinion that a bill of rights for electronic citizens is 
unnecessary. Aren't the powers of educational institutions already 
limited? Other educators argue persuasively that rights for electronic 
citizens must be clearly spelled out in order to ensure individual 
rights. The draft bill represents a starting point for debate that might 
continue for at least the same 200 years' time that debate has 
surrounded the original Bill of Rights. With conflict between individual 
rights and institutional responsibilities inevitable, a process for 
resolving such conflicts must be put in place. With regard to the 
electronic community, it will be interesting to see what principle 
bulwark of the rights of electronic citizens versus the responsibilities 
of educational institutions evolves. Currently, EDUCOM, CAUSE, the 
Coalition for Networked Information, and the Electronic Frontier 
Foundation all play a role, as do other special interest groups. Perhaps 
these organizations will be viewed as power centers that expand 
alternately the rights of individuals and the rights of institutions, 
much as the Supreme Court is viewed. Whatever the body, the resolution 
of conflicts between individual rights in the electronic environment and 
institutional responsibilities will be resolved as a political process.

Endnotes
     1. E. A. Cavazes. "Computer bulletin board systems and the right of 
reply: Redefining defamation liability for a new technology." Review of 
Litigation, 12:231-48, 1992.
     2. L. Murdock. "The use and abuse of computerized information: 
Striking a balance between personal privacy interests and organizational 
needs." In S. Lipner and S. Kalman, eds., Computer Law, pp. 480-84. 
Columbus, OH: Merrill Publishing, 1989.
     3. John Shattuck. "Computer matching is a serious threat to 
individual rights." In Charles Dunlop and Rob Kling, eds., 
Computerization and Controversy, pp. 439-45. San Diego: Academic Press, 
1991.






