Date: Wed, 23 Jun 1993 16:51:05 GMT

Reprinted from EDUCOM Review, Volume 28, Number 3, May/June 1993. 
Contact: EDUCOM, 1112 16th Street, NW, Suite 600, Washington, DC 20036; 
202 872-4200; via e-mail: EDITOR@BITNIC.EDUCOM.EDU

A Code of Rights and Responsibilities for Access to Computing Resources

By Robert S. Tannenbaum

I enthusiastically volunteered to guest-edit this issue of EDUCOM Review 
devoted to the emerging Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for 
Electronic Learners because we at the University of Kentucky were deeply 
engaged in developing a code of rights and responsibilities for all 
users of our computing resources, and because I see these efforts as 
having enormous potential value for the academic community. As I write 
this column, the final draft of our University code, written by the task 
force that I chair and approved by various faculty and student 
leadership groups on the campus, is awaiting presentation to the 
President's Cabinet. If it is approved by that body, it will be added to 
the University's Administrative Regulations to become official 
University of Kentucky policy.1
     During the course of its deliberations, the University of Kentucky 
task force gathered information from a very large number of colleges and 
universities regarding their ethics policies and the suggestions that 
they could offer us based on their experiences in developing them. We 
found and joined several listservs on the Internet. We spoke with many 
helpful and sympathetic colleagues all across the country. The paper 
materials that we gathered fill two large notebooks. The electronic 
materials fill several cylinders. Having gathered all of that 
information, we saw how obviously valuable the Bill of Rights and 
Responsibilities for Electronic Learners would be for us and for others. 
     We, and many colleagues with whom we spoke, felt the urgent need 
for an authoritative statement of underlying principles and ethics that 
could serve as the foundation for our local policy and code of behavior. 
But the development and adoption of such a fundamental statement require 
input from countless sources, seemingly endless discussion and revision, 
and volunteer effort by hundreds of dedicated professionals. The end 
result, however, will give us a widely accepted statement of principles 
that we and others can cite as a primary authority for our own efforts, 
much as we now cite EDUCOM's Guide to the Ethical and Legal Use of 
Software for Members of the Academic Community.
     When I returned from EDUCOM'92 with the draft of the Bill of Rights 
and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners and the information that 
this was an EDUCOM project, it lent at once credibility and support to 
our local efforts. Our activities and thinking were seen as timely, 
relevant, and mainstream. Although we had organized our policy 
differently, our draft dealt with both the rights and the 
responsibilities of both the individual users and the University, just 
as did the draft of the bill. Our underlying philosophy was in concert 
with that expressed in the bill. Our premises and conclusions were 
confirmed. Such external, peer confirmation is extraordinarily 
important, almost essential, in matters as fundamental to the life of 
the University as the policy for access to and use of computing 
resources.
     I find in our local efforts at the University of Kentucky both a 
need for and a strong affirmation of the national activities at EDUCOM. 
Our draft policy begins with a statement of our underlying philosophy 
and the five basic principles of the policy:
     "Access to computing resources is granted to an individual by the 
University of Kentucky solely for the grantee's own use. Every user of 
the University of Kentucky computing resources has two basic rights 
regarding computing--privacy and a fair share of resources. It is 
unethical and a violation of this policy for any person to violate these 
rights.
     All users, in turn, are expected to exercise common sense and 
decency (due regard for the rights of others) with respect to the public 
computing resources, thereby reflecting the spirit of community and 
intellectual inquiry at the University. Access is a right that may be 
limited or revoked if an individual misuses the right or violates 
applicable University policies or state or federal laws.
     Principles Governing Use of Computing Resources:
     1. User access is granted to an individual and may not be 
transferred to or shared with another without explicit written 
authorization from the vice president for Information Systems, a 
designee, or the appropriate system administrator.
     2. User access to computing resources is contingent upon prudent 
and responsible use.
     3. The user may not use computing resources for any illegal or 
unauthorized act; in particular, the user may not use computing 
resources to violate any state or federal laws or any of the regulations 
specified in the Governing Regulations, the Administrative Regulations, 
the Student Rights and Responsibilities handbook (Code of Student 
Conduct), the Rules of the University Senate, the Faculty Code, the 
University System Faculty Handbook, or the Staff Handbook.
     4. The user may not use computing resources for any commercial 
purpose without prior written authorization from the vice president for 
Information Systems, a designee, or the appropriate system 
administrator. 
     5. Computing resources must be shared among users in an equitable 
manner. The user may not participate in any behavior that unreasonably 
interferes with the fair use of computing resources by another."
     If the Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners 
had been completed and adopted by EDUCOM, we almost certainly would have 
referred to it, quoted portions, or even reproduced the entire document 
as part of our policy.
     We went beyond the bill to include ten examples of possible 
violations of the policy, in an effort to inform our community of users 
of the meaning and implications of our policy, not to intimidate or 
constrain. Our policy concludes with statements regarding sanctions and 
enforcement that were painstakingly crafted in an attempt to be at once 
fair, nonpunative, discouraging of violations, and practical to enforce. 
A glossary is appended to define all of the terms used in the policy 
both to educate naive users and to make the policy as unambiguous as 
possible. These extensions beyond the Bill of Rights and 
Responsibilities for Electronic Learners are entirely in keeping with 
the intent of its framers: "As is true with the EDUCOM [software] Code, 
we expect the end product to be widely distributed, referred to, and 
used as a guide by organizations and individuals as they develop policy 
and personnel statements."2
     This issue of EDUCOM Review is intended as a further introduction 
of the Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners for 
the academic community, as a forum for discussion of several of the 
fundamental issues raised by the bill, and as an invitation for as many 
others as possible to become involved in debating these issues and their 
implications. It is my hope that from this involvement and this debate 
there will come a final version of the bill that will be adopted by 
EDUCOM as official policy, that will be widely distributed, and that 
will serve as inspiration for other local efforts such as ours at the 
University of Kentucky. It is hoped that this final version can be 
completed at EDUCOM'93 and formally adopted as soon thereafter as 
possible.
     The articles in this issue of EDUCOM Review that are devoted to the 
Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners were 
selected to provide both an introduction to the bill itself and a 
discussion of several of the most fundamental issues related to it. The 
articles are also intended to initiate a discourse on the many issues, 
both philosophical and practical, associated with the bill.
     * Connolly and Webster introduce the bill, explaining the 
circumstances under which it was developed, the plans for its 
completion, and the hopes for its future uses and relationship to other 
bills for other disciplines.
     * A fundamental assumption of modern higher education is that we 
are preparing people to continue to grow and learn throughout their 
lives--a "learning society." Both Webster and Connolly, and Connolly and 
Schneebeck in their articles explore this key concept of a "community of 
electronic learners" and the implications of the bill for this community 
and for lifelong learning
     * At the forefront of most discussions of computing, especially 
networked systems, are the questions of security and privacy of 
information--how do we define these, how do we guarantee them, who is 
responsible for them? Spetalnick discusses this important concept of 
privacy and issues related to privacy in an electronic environment.
     * Throughout the history of society, people have had to consider 
the relationship of the individual to the group and the limitations that 
must be placed on the one for the benefit of the other. Clearly, this 
balancing of rights and limitations works in both directions: both 
individuals and groups must be limited in order for the other to 
benefit. Stager begins the major task of delineating and confronting the 
potential conflicts between personal rights (such as privacy) and the 
responsibilities of institutions to provide and maintain electronic 
resources for all users.
     * No discussion of computing in our society could be complete 
unless it considers matters of enfranchisement of all citizens. Van 
Bergen confronts us with some of the serious philosophical and practical 
uses related to an electronic community vis a vis women and other 
groups. 
     These are but a few of the many subjects that need to be considered 
in the ongoing discussion of the bill. It is hoped that many other 
individuals will become part of this discourse, contributing letters, 
articles, electronic memoranda, position papers, and personal 
participation in conference panels and discussions. It is only by the 
active involvement of a significant number of our colleagues that we can 
hope to arrive at a workable consensus on such an important document, 
one that may color our professional lives for years to come.

Endnote
     1. I am pleased to report that the proposed policy was approved by 
the President's Cabinet on March 18, 1993 and is now part of the 
Administrative Regulations of the University of Kentucky.
     2. "The Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic 
Learners." EDUCOM Review 28: (2), 1993.





