In article <205j2t$blb@geraldo.cc.utexas.edu>, jah@mojo.ots.utexas.edu (Jeff
Hayward) says:
>
>Do you know if the Doe standard applies to all university regulations
>or just those with a strong constitutionally protected component,
>speech codes, for example?
>
>That is; does the same standard of proof apply to (a) cheating on an
>exam, (b) dumping soap in the fountain, (c) having alcohol at a dorm
>party, (d) posting anti-semitic material to usenet?

The judicial standard of proof varies from school to school as well
as within the school for different types of processes.  For instance,
at UMich, Carl tells us, the standard is "clear and convincing," while
atUMaryland it used to be "preponderance of the evidence," but is now
"clear and convincing evidence."  (Actually, I think that at Michigan
there may be several standrds, because their judicial process is
highly decentralized.  Can anyone help me out here?)

Except in unusual cases, the standard of proof will not be set by
a higher authority, but by the institution's own rules: the Student
Conduct Code, or whatever.  If the overall rules establish distinct
processes for academic dishonesty and physical violence offenses, for
example, then the two sets of rules may set different standards of
proof.

In addition, different types of offense may trigger different levels
of due process.  The judicially imposed rule of thumb, and a quite
reasonable one IMHO, is that more serious potential sanctions rrequire
greater due process protections.  So, your example of dumping soap in
the fountain, so long as it was not done by a "repeat offender" would
probably (if the system provided for it) be handled in a less-formal
"disciplinary conference" rather than given a formal hearing, since
it would not likely result in suspension or expulsion.  (_Goss v.
Lopez_, 419 U.S. 565 (1975)--avaliable for anon ftp from ftp.eff.org
in (I think) /pub/academic .)

Those who have followed the UH case here will note that the general
principle I cite *appears* to be among those not follower strictly.
Judging from what has been reported to us, UH violated badly basic
due process requirements and their own rules.  (Unless James/"KC"
willingly gave up the right to a hearing and selected a conference,
I can't understand why it went the process route that it did.  Even
if he *wanted* a conference, if I were the judicial officer I'd have
gone with a hearing if I thought there was a chance he'd be suspended.)

Bruce Umbaugh
