From caf-talk Caf Jun 24 12:34:03 1993
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Re: [EDUCOM] Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for Electronic Learners
Date: 24 Jun 1993 12:33:39 -0400
Message-ID: <20cl13$kr4@eff.org>

>[Reprinted with permission from EDUCOM Review, Volume 28, Number 3,
>May/June 1993.
[...]
>     In order to protect the rights and recognize the responsibilities 
>of individuals and institutions, we the members of the educational 
>community propose this Bill of Rights and Responsibilities for 
>Electronic Learners. 
[...]

Summary: I think this is great. My main concern, perhaps pedantic, is
that it is not as clearly worded as the U.S. Bill of Rights. Also, in
many places the wording implicitly suggests that rights are given
rather than inherent. To me, something that is given is, at best, an
entitlement not a right. See references for several "bills of rights",
that I've used as models.

>Article I: Individual Rights
>     Section 1. A citizen's access to computing and information 
>resources is a right. Access to computing or information resources shall 
>not be denied or removed without just cause.

>     Section 2. The right to access includes the right to appropriate 
>training and the tools required to effect access.

These two could be expressed more concisely:

   The right of a citizen to computing and information resources,
   including appropriate training and tools, shall not be denied or
   removed without just cause.

>     Section 3. All citizens shall have the right to be informed about 
>personal information that is being and has been collected about them, 
>the right to review and correct that information, and the right to 
>control the distribution of that information beyond the expressed 
>purpose of its collection. 

The phrase "shall have the right" suggests that this right is not
inherent. I suggest:

   Section 3. Citizen shall be informed about personal information
   that is being and has been collected about them. They shall be
   allowed to review and correct that information. ...

With either wording the last point is unclear. Is their ability to
control distribution "opt in" or "opt out"? I suggest that "opt in" be
make explicit:

   ... Personal information about a citizen shall not be used for
   other than the expressed purpose of its collection without the
   expressed permission of that citizen.

>     Section 4. The constitutional right to freedom of speech applies to 
>citizens of electronic communities just as it does to citizens of other 
>communities.

The U.S. Constitution guarantees that the government and government
agents will not abridge freedom of speech. It does not apply, for
example, to private universities and therefore it cannot be used as
the basis a freedom of speech right in all of academia. I suggest:

   Discussion and expression shall be permitted subject only to
   requirements for the maintenance of order. Citizens shall be free
   from institutional censorship.

>     Section 5. All citizens of the electronic community of learners 
>have ownership rights over their own intellectual works.

>Article II: Individual Responsibilities
[...]

Rather than quote each item in "individual responsibilities", I'll quote
an example:

>     ... it shall be each citizen's personal 
>responsibility to verify the integrity and completeness 
>of information that he or she compiles or uses.

What does this mean? Does it mean that a citizen can be punished for
not verifing the integrity of some data that he or she uses or does it
mean that if a citizen doesn't verify and something bad happens then
he or she is responsible?

>     Section 4. Each citizen, as a member of the electronic community of 
>learners, is responsible to all other citizens in that community: to 
[...]
>respect the diversity of the population in the community; ...

I don't know what this means. I would guess that it mean either 1)
nothing or 2) you can be punished for "hate speech". If it means
nothing it should be removed because it adds nothing.  If it prohibits
"hate speech" it should be removed as contrary to academic freedom
(and constitutional law). [See refs].

> .. to behave ethically;

This is much too vague. It seems to suggest that if I send a lie to my
wife via email (an unethical act), the university could punish me.

It probably means to refer to some code of academic or professional
ethics. I think that it would be better just to mention that a
prohibited activity (e.g. cheating on homework) is, of course, still
prohibited when done on a computer.


>Article III: Rights of Educational Institutions

[...]
>     Section 1. Educational institutions' access to computing resources 
>and information is a right rather than a privilege. Access to computing 
>resources and information shall not be denied or removed without just 
>cause.

The word "privilege" hear seems to mean "a permission that can be
withdrawn arbitrarily". This is not what it usually means in the U.S.
Constitution or in the law; rather it often means "a right applicable
only to some citizens" (for example, senators are privileged from
arrest during their attendance in the Senate) or it means an
entitlement (which still usually has due process requirements).

I suggest this wording:

   The right of an educational institution to computing and
   information resources shall not be denied or removed without just
   cause.

[...]
>     Section 3. Educational institutions have the right to allocate 
>resources in line with their unique institutional missions.

This is not a right. It is an authorization:

     Section 3. An educational institution has authority to allocate
     resources in line with its institutional mission.

