elee9sf@menudo.menudo.UH.EDU (Karl Barrus) wrote:
>
>I guess the concern here is how much circumstantial evidence it takes
>to really focus suspicion on a user.  Is there ever a point where
>circumstantial evidence alone is good enough to file charges?

No, but there is a point at which circumstantial evidence is enough
to warrant more intrusive measures (file/data searches, et cetera).
Of course, that shouldn't be the admins' decision to make; approval
should be sought from the appropriate authority.

>I mean really, crack is available at bunches of anonymous ftp sites.
>Is merely having a copy in your account enough to focus suspicion?  Is
>it enough to file charges?  (In these cases assume that the user in
>question was not actually seen running crack.)

We're descending into the biggest conflict in (enlightened) systems 
administration -- balancing the users' privacy/confidentiality against 
the admins' obligation to provide a reasonably secure, effective compu- 
ting environment.

If we assume that we don't have *hard* evidence of a user running Crack
(such as accounting records or ps logs), how do we even gather circum-
stantial evidence without violating user privacy/confidentiality?

If we are to be committed to user privacy, we cannot perform any such
gathering.  If, on the other hand, we commit ourselves to user *confi-
dentiality* (a subtle, but significant, change) we can proceed.  I do
not believe that we can guarantee privacy in a multi-user environment;
confidentiality is the best we can do.  (That's another thread....)

It's a tough call to make; I think we'll all agree on that.  However, I
have to believe that my obligations to (in my case) 2000+ users over-
rides the individual user's privacy/confidentiality.  There are those
who suggest that absolutely nothing should be done until an offense is
actually committed; I don't agree with that.  My obligation to provide
computing service *demands* that I take active steps to prevent lapses
in system security.

What do I do?  Oh, I don't do anything truly invasive, but I do use some
of the tools at my command.  I've been known to use /etc/fuser just to
see if anyone is accessing /etc/passwd from a process other than a sys-
tem program; if jbuser01's program "snarfle" has burned up 200 CPU min-
utes while keeping /etc/passwd open, I'm going to be curious and ask him
about it.  (If it's also opening up /usr/dict/words, I'm going to become
*really* curious.)  I should mention that I've never seen this particular
scenario; it's a well-publicized fact that cracking software isn't allowed
on our systems, and my users seem to be respecting that.  8)

--Wes

