In the U.S., at a state university, your .plan would probably be
constitutional protected. At a private university, it would depend on
the schools rules, but the official policy of many private schools is
that speech is protected.

In the case of U.S. state schools the principle is that the there is
no right not to be offended (although there is a right not to be
harassed), but there is a right to freedom of expression in
government-owned forums where free expression has been allowed.

In both state and private schools another principle is that free
expression, even offensive expression, is central to the mission of
the university.

I don't know the law in Australia. Could the university stop a student
newspaper from publishing rude articles? If not, why isn't computer
expression given the same protection. If so, who is authorized to
censor? At the very least computer censorship should require the same
level of authorization as other forms of censorship.

As to the idea that "they own the computer, they can do anything they
want" or "computer use is just a privilege". An academic institution
should work within its charter toward its mission. To act arbitrarily
violates likely violates its charter and hurts its mission.

- Carl
