Joe Thiebes said that "getting into the *good graces* of the admins"
sounds like "kissing up to admins."  That isn't quite what I envi-
sioned, but I can see where it would look that way.......

I, for one, don't want anyone kissing up to me; I don't have time
for that kind of nonsense.  What I do really appreciate is users 
keeping me aware of potential problems.  If you're going to build
or run some code that's going to take days, let your admin know!  
(When folks are running code for days for finals/theses/etc., I've 
been known to postpone reboots to let it run.)  If you're wondering 
if certain packages (Crack, games, MUDs, whatever) will/would get 
you in hot water, ask!  If someone else needs an account (and is 
entitled to one), have them contact the admin! (don't just give them 
your password)  If someone's harassing you via email, let us know; we 
can tell you how to contact their admins or file a complaint with the 
appropriate people.  (An admin usually isn't the appropriate person)

It really takes cooperation.  We (admins) can't make arbitrary policy
(or policy decisions) if we want to provide optimum service to you.
On the other hand, you can't just take/do whatever you want without (in
many cases) causing problems for other users and forcing us to act on
our obligation to ALL users.  It's a balancing act, and users are half 
the pyramid.  I think that most admins will make arrangements for most
user needs, but we have to be informed.

