To sum the situation up in so few words could be misleading; the actual
happenings were more complex.

It has not been my policy to question requests for accounts when
accompanied by the proper approvals.  KC presented to me (actually my
assistant) a properly filled-out form.

Now we run a relatively small system (ten Suns, a few RS6Ks and Irises, and
a supercomputer) for the purposes of the department, mainly research and
general connectivity.  We do not generally support the computing needs of
undergraduates; the campus computing centers handle that.  We make
exceptions for undergraduates working with professors on special projects.
We do not grant class accounts.

Simply stated, the professor who signed the form did not understand this.
A small mistake; certainly one undergrad isn't going to choke the system.
Normally this would have gone completely unnoticed.  Unfortunately it
didn't, since KC broke the rules.  Actually he only bent them by using a
system that he shouldn't have been using.  /etc/motd warns users not to use
this system except in cases of extreme system load or when certain things
need to be done (it's the file server, but some disks are not exported).

Someone noticed KC using the machine.  Now word does get around the campus,
especially to department computing committee members.  The person who
noticed KS is one such member.  This person had also read that posting that
mentioned all past charges against KC _and_ seemingly unrelated incidents
of password cracking on campus.  Top it off with the fact that KC had an
open telnet session to a site in Germany and one can understand why the
alarms were sounded.  It gets worse when one sees the .plan file that the
professor was presented with when he fingered djkc@math.uh.edu.

As far as I know nobody called up "the math dept" and had the account shut
down.  I would be somewhat upset if this was the case, since I am the
person that should have been contacted.  (After, you call the
administrator, not the bureaucrat, to report a possible security problem
right?)

So that's what happened from my perspective.  I will sum up by saying that
if KC had obeyed the (extremely simple) rules and had just laid low for a
while nobody would have noticed and he wouldn't once again be without the
privilege of net access.

