CAMPUS REPORT
June 1993

CAMPUS BRIEFS

Scouts Get Court Support
     The Boy Scouts can legally exclude atheists, ruled a federal
appeals court in Chicago.  The court said that the national
organization could deny admittance to a ten-year-old boy whose
father refused to let him recite the group's pledge of duty to
God and country.
     The seventh circuit court ruled in a 2-1 decision that
because the Boy Scouts is a private organization, it is not a
place where the public must be accommodated and it is not
required by any civil rights law to admit agnostics or atheists.
     A similar California state court ruling is currently under
appeal.  According to The Washington Times, another Chicago
atheist has filed a suit to ban Boy Scout activities at publicly
funded schools.
     This is the first time the issue has reached the federal
appeals court level, a Boy Scouts spokesman said.

Frat Party Had First Amendment Protection
     Another federal appeals court ruled that an "ugly-woman
contest" held by a George Mason University fraternity was
protected by the constitution.  Justices for the fourth circuit
court upheld a lower court ruling that while the performance may
have been offensive, it was still covered by the First Amendment.
     The skit "was an exercise of teenage campus excess," wrote
Judge James M. Sprouse.  "[But] low quality of entertainment does
not necessarily weigh in the First Amendment inquiry," the Judge
also wrote.
     Sigma Chi fraternity put on the skit in April of 1991.  It
featured members dressed in drag including one in blackface.  GMU
officials deemed it racist and sexist and placed the fraternity
on probation, prompting Sigma Chi to sue the university.  Members
of the fraternity were required to attend racial-sensitivity
programs sponsored by Sigma Chi and a campus sorority.

UCLA Caves In to Strikers
     A 14-day hunger strike by Hispanic students at UCLA ended
when the administration caved in to political pressure and met
several of the strikers demands including the allotment of an
additional $800,000 for the Chicano Studies program.
     Nine students and faculty members started the hunger strike
in May after hundreds of Hispanic UCLA students went on a rampage
after a violent demonstration, causing between $35,000 and
$50,000 worth of damage to the school's faculty center.  The
students were protesting the university's decision not to raise
the Chicano Studies program there to departmental level.
     UCLA chancellor Charles E. Young said last month that the
program was preferable in its current, interdisciplinary state
because it brings together professors from different academic
fields.  But with the health of the strikers rapidly
deteriorating, Young provided new funding for six full time
professors to be hired over the next two years and he renamed the
program "The Cesar Chavez Center for Interdisciplinary
Instruction in Chicana and Chicano Studies."
     The Los Angeles Times called the funding increase
"impressive" because UCLA is planning to drop its nursing program
and other programs due to budget restraints.
     In another concession, 84 of 99 students were arrested and
charged with felony counts of vandalism trespassing were let off
the hook.  Though UCLA's executive vice chancellor, Andrea L.
Rich, condemned the violence "in the strongest terms," calling
the property destruction "inappropriate and unacceptable,"
administrators decided to continue with the prosecution of only
seven of the protesters.  In addition, university attorneys said
they may also drop charges against the seven if they agree to pay
$30,000 of the damages to the faculty center.
     The hunger strikers' demand that the government of Mexico
reclaim the western part of the United States which they claim
was stolen from Mexico by white settlers was not addressed in the
settlement.

Prof. Punished for Student's Speech

     A tenured journalism instructor at Santa Rosa Junior College
was placed on administrative leave last month after he "allowed"
students to write offensive messages on a computer bulletin board
he built and administered.  The school is trying to determine
whether messages on computer networks are protected by the First
Amendment.
     "We very well could have entered a whole new era," SRJC
president Robert Agrella told The Chronicle of Higher Education.
"Can anyone write anything they want to?  Basically, I think the
answer is Yes with pencil and paper.  Is it different on a
computer?  I don't know," he said.
     Two female students filed a sexual harassment complaint with
the college charging that messages written by male students about
them were obscene and sexist and that the professor, Roger
Karraker, was responsible for them.
     The messages were posted in a file called "Men Only" which
Karraker had established along with one for "Women Only" to
encourage private discussions of gender issues.  Access to the
files was limited to students who signed up and agreed to keep
the discussions confidential.
     Five male students responsible for the messages or for
divulging them to one of the female students were denied access
to the bulletin board and the Men Only and Women Only sections
were eliminated immediately, Karraker said.
     In suspending the instructor, SRJC officials indicated they
did not feel Karraker had done enough to censor messages sent on
the system.  But the instructor disagrees over his roll as a
bulletin board administrator.
     "The personnel director sees it in terms of an editor who
checks everything that goes in the newspaper," Karraker told the
Chronicle.  "I see it more like a telephone system, and if
someone calls up and makes a threat or uses abusive language, you
don't shut down the phone system.  You go after the person who
made the call."

