CAMPUS REPORT
June 1993

CAMPUS REPORT EDITORIAL

     Back in June of 1988, Campus Report exposed Leonard
Jeffries, the former chairman of black studies at City College of
New York, as a racist who told his introductory classes that
whites are biologically inferior to blacks and that "If I had my
way, I'd wipe them off the face of the earth."
     Jeffries also told his classes that the explosion of the
space shuttle Challenger was probably the best thing to happen to
America in a long time.  He asserted that "the space program
needed something to slow it down to keep white people from
spreading their filth throughout the universe."
     The professor later told his class and Campus Report that he
didn't really want white people wiped from the face of the Earth,
only the white values system.
     At the time, CCNY president Dennard W. Harleston told a
student press conference that he was "personally troubled and
deeply concerned" about articles in a campus newspaper reporting
Jeffries' comments.  "I am concerned because the views and
comments reported in the articles have the potential--at the
minimum--to misrepresent City College and what this institution
stands for," he said.
     After that, things died down for Jeffries until 1991 when he
commented in a two-hour speech to the Empire State Black Arts and
Cultural Festival that whites are "pathological," "dirty," and
"dastardly, devilish folk."  He alleged that Jews and the Mafia
were responsible for a conspiracy to portray blacks negatively in
Hollywood productions.  And he further accused the Jews of
financing the slave trade.
     But Jeffries is best known for his infamous theory of the
"Sun People" and the "Ice People."
     "Our thesis is that the 'sun people,' the African family of
warm communal hope, meets an antithesis, the vision of 'ice
people,' Europeans, colonizers, oppressors, the cold, rigid
elements in world history," Jeffries was quoted in Newsday.
     "The values system of the sun is the civilizing value
system, the value system of the ice is barbaric.... Twenty to
forty thousand years in the caves of Europe, that's the
foundation of the value system of Europe--a male-dominated cave
man value system," he later told Campus Report.
     Well, despite all of his alleged disgust for the white man's
icy values system, the ever-hypocritical Jeffries used that
system--specifically its justice system--to make himself $400,000
richer at the cost of the taxpayers of New York.
     After Jeffries' 1991 speech, he was demoted by the CCNY
administration from chairman of the black studies department to a
full professor there.  He took no cut in pay and continued to
inflict his racist ravings on group after group of mostly lower
class students.  The university cited mismanagement of the
department during Jeffries 19 years as chairman as reasoning for
the demotion.
     Jeffries, however, claimed the college was infringing on his
first amendment rights--punishing him for his '91 speech and the
controversy it had stirred.  Though he suffered no financial
setback from the demotion, he sued City College for $25 million
in damages.
     The jury decided he hadn't been damaged that much.  Though
some jurors wished to award Jeffries as much as $1 million, the
group settled on a $400,000 figure divided between six
defendants.  One juror, Robert Mercer, told the New York Times
that he wished Jeffries' hadn't gotten any of the money.  He said
he felt the administration deserved to be punished, "but it would
have been nice if someone could have given money to [the] black
studies department or to charity."  He regretted that Jeffries
may benefit monetarily from the case, the Times said.
     We regret this too.  Though the damages were assigned to
four trustees and two administrators, including City University
of New York chancellor Ann Reynolds, the college itself will have
to cough up the money because the six were acting under the
auspices of the school.  And it is doubtful that the $400,000
will come from the black studies budget.  More likely, a program
like science or nursing will suffer for the cause of victimology-
-just as the nursing program at UCLA is being nixed to fund the
newly elevated "Cesar Chavez Center for Interdisciplinary
Instruction of Chicana and Chicano Studies" [See Campus Briefs,
page 3].
     Jeffries' hypocrisy is typical of Leftist radicals.  He
rants against the supposed immorality of the American government
and justice system then uses it for personal gain.  Certainly,
any American citizen has the right to do this--our country would
be in serious trouble if they did not.  But this is not the type
of role-model college students need.  This is not the type of
"teacher" who belongs anywhere near a classroom.   It is the
students at CCNY who are being hurt the most--by Jeffries
comments--and his lawsuits.
     The administration says it will appeal.  Hopefully the next
jury will not feel so compelled to punish CCNY and its students
for Leonard Jeffries' personal gain.
