CAMPUS REPORT
June 1993

OFF THE SHELF
(GREAT BOOKS YOU WON'T FIND ON THE SYLLABUS)

Prudence: the Governing Principle for Conservatives
Intercollegiate Studies Institute, $12.95

Review by Kipling Oren

     For years, Russell Kirk has been the conscience of the
conservative movement.  His first major work, The Conservative
Mind, published originally in 1953, was vastly influential and
revived the conservative movement in the U.S.  This book and
others by Kirk are a must read for young conservatives, and his
latest work is no exception.
     The Politics of Prudence is a compilation of essays--listing
several principles, events and players--that the young
conservative should find to be very useful.  Prudence is easily
understood and simpler than some of Kirk's other scholarly works.
In the early chapters of the book, Kirk lists several principles
to which traditional conservatives adhere.  In these chapters he
stresses, among other things, the errors of ideology over what he
terms the "permanent things."  In other chapters, the author
defends traditional principles while discussing events and
contemporary issues important to modern America.
     Young conservatives may find Kirk's view of ideology to be
surprising.  like John Adams, Kirk finds ideology to be "the
science of idiocy."  In Prudence, Kirk describes the birth of
ideology in the 1800s:
     "Rejecting religion and metaphysics, the original ideologues
believed that they could discover a system of natural laws--which
system, if conformed to, could become the foundation of universal
harmony and contentment.... Late-born children of the dying
Enlightenment, the Ideologues assumed that systematized knowledge
derived from sensation could perfect society through ethical and
educational methods and by well-organized political direction."
     Kirk explains that ideology is "a political formula that
promises mankind an earthly paradise; but in cruel fact what
ideology has created is a series of terrestrial hells."  The
conservative, on the other hand, is governed by prudence:  he
recognizes the imperfectibility of man while looking to tried and
true methods rather than untried methods which can be potentially
dangerous.  Kirk lists several principles by which the
conservative should abide.  In sum, his definition of a
conservative is one whose views are founded on convention,
custom, and "the long experience of the human species."  The
author is openly nervous about a younger generation, claiming to
be conservative, whose actions, in practice, make them dangerous
"idealogues"--their thoughts and actions are founded merely on
ideas.  Kirk tells the reader that such individuals "have no
notion of prudence, temperance, compromise, the traditions of
civility, or cultural patrimony."
     Since the author of Prudence attaches such importance to
tradition and custom, it is altogether fitting that he should
mention several events influenced by the "conservative impulse"
and thus important to the modern conservative movement.  Kirk
explains:
     "I am concerned with the conflict between the forces of
integration and those of disintegration.  By the conservative
impulse, I mean the inclination to support a venerable moral
order, an established social order, a society of voluntary
community, and such healthful institutions as private property
and representative government."
     Not surprisingly, in using this criteria, Kirk finds events
like the ratification of the U.S. Constitution, the victories of
Benjamin Disraeli in Victorian England, the defeat of the Nazis
in 1945, and the election of Ronald Reagan to be the most
important events of the last seven generations.
     From his vast understanding of traditional principles, Kirk
tackles several issues important to today's conservative
movement.  Kirk compares the principles of conservatives to those
of the libertarian.  He again hearkens to the traditional
principle of prudence and contrasts this with the libertarian's
insatiable love for liberty.  Throughout, Prudence is rich with
quotes from great figures and his essay on libertarians is no
exception.  In criticizing libertarians, the author quotes
Dostoevski, who wrote, "To begin with unlimited freedom is to end
with unlimited despotism."  Kirk calls for order when he writes,
"The worst enemies of enduring freedom for all may be certain
folk who demand incessantly more liberty for themselves."
     Other topics of interest to the young conservative might be
Kirk's dispassionate scourging of the so-called Neoconservatives
whose main vice, according to the author, is their reliance on
ideology over custom.  In one essay, the author gives an in-depth
report on the state of modern popular conservatism.  In Prudence,
Kirk also gives the reader his views on foreign policy,
education, and popular government.
     In his chapter on education, Kirk discusses what he calls
the decadence of today's higher learning.  Kirk calls for
American universities to return to their original purpose:  "To
discipline the mind; to give men and women long views and to
instill in them the virtue of prudence; to present a coherent
body of ordered knowledge, in several great fields; to pursue
that knowledge for its own sake; to help the rising generation to
make its way toward wisdom and virtue."  In short, the goal of
the university should be to teach that truth is better than
falsehood, and that wisdom is better than ignorance.
     Kirk also mentions other important books and players in the
conservative movement.  One chapter, titled "Ten Conservative
Books," mentions works like Alexis de Tocqueville's Democracy in
America which, because of the vast knowledge they contain, have
had an incredible effect on the history of the United States.
Kirk also discusses the influence of more modern writers like
T.S. Eliot, Donald Davidson, Malcolm Muggeridge, and Wilhelm
Roepke.
     The overriding theme for the conservative is prudence.
Russell Kirk, through his writings and lectures, has exemplified
this trait and the young conservative would do well to learn from
his example.

