MAIL BAG

Another University Corrupted by PC

     Today our society suffers from many different social ills.
Among them are abortion, crime, divorce, drugs, and
homosexuality.  All of these have had a negative influence on the
moral standards of American society, as well as society itself.
But the growing influence of homosexuals is by far the most
disturbing.
     Today, our children are not educated about reading, writing
and arithmetic, but about condoms, sexually transmitted diseases
and gay sex.  Children today no longer read Dr. Seuss, but Gloria
Goes to Gay Pride and Daddy's Roommate instead.  Today, educators
are more concerned with teaching our children how to thrive in a
secular society than they are with teaching them right from
wrong.
     How did we ever get to such a point?  The God-fearing people
of this country stood by silently as the liberals on the Supreme
Court, in the Congress and in the states restricted religious
freedom to the extent that all references to God and morality are
now unconstitutional.  "Religion is at the root of morality; and
morality is the basis of law," said Pat Buchanan.  What will
become the new basis of our laws when religion and morality are
viewed as irrelevant and unconstitutional?  What will our society
be like?
     We need not go out into the urban, secular jungles of
America to find this decay and decadence which threatens to
destroy our great nation.  We can see it here at Stetson
University in Florida, where a small, Southern Baptist
university, once committed to Judeo-Christian and Western values
is now under siege by heterophobic, radical homosexuals who
vandalize the campus with pro-homosexual graffiti and obscenity
and where the Student Government Association, in an effort to
deal with STDs, recently passed a bill urging the administration
to put condom vending machines in almost all bathrooms on campus.
     Rather than promote abstinence, the only sure way to avoid
getting sexually transmitted diseases, or force people to be
responsible for their own conduct, the SGA thought it best to
spend the little amount of mandatory student fees they control on
condoms.
     But that's not all.  A week later the same organization
passed a bill banning homophobic remarks as forms of sexual
harassment.  Under that bill, I could be disciplined by the
university for writing or saying "dyke, fag, or queer."  No
matter that such speech codes imposed on the student body at the
University of Michigan were struck down by a federal court last
year; we here at Stetson seek to be a diverse, accepting and
progressive student body.  NOT!
     The end result of such absurd legislation would be divisive,
oppressive and regressive as it unconstitutionally infringes upon
the First Amendment guarantees of the students here at Stetson
and of all the citizens of this country.

Bill Moeller
Ocala, FL
