tenured professor.  During a recent keynote address at the City
University of New York(CUNY), Berthoff used Rodney's "Can we all
get along" statements from his post L.A. riot press confab last
year as a model of "non-linear concept formation" that creative
writing teachers should use with their students.
     "King's bursts of eloquence, his balance between image and
topic, particular and universal, reveals a mind and heart that
are engaged by their topic," said Berthoff, adding that his use
of language prompts listeners to "ask how come we all cannot work
it out.'
     Berthoff also called attention to King's "interactive
movement between personal and public" that should be encouraged
in all students' writing.  As proof of King's semantic skills,
she presented an excerpt from the speech that included: "You
know, I mean we're all stuck here for a while.  Let's, you know,
let's try to work it out.  Let's work it out."

***
     To Your Health? Harvard Prez Neil Rudenstine has announced
that the school plans to extend health insurance coverage to
homosexual faculty and staff members in "same-sex domestic
partnerships" and also offer health insurance coverage to the
children of those couples.
     Couples who wish to qualify must register their partnerships
in a city such as Cambridge, which has recognized same-sex
partnerships as marriage ever since last year.  They must also
sign a document affirming their mutual financial commitment and
notify the school "promptly" if the partnership ends, according
to a Reuters story.
     Among the handful of schools that offer similar coverage are
MIT, Stanford, Clark U. and the U. of Chicago.  As yet, Harvard
greybeards have no idea how many of their employees will sign up
for this benefit, although a recent guesstimate hovered in the
area of 1 percent.

