     Hetero Centerfold: Rutgers U. feminist prof Catharine
Stimpson was fit to be tied, according to the Chronicle of Higher
Education, when that naughty rag Heterodoxy featured her as "Ms.
April" in its recent issue.  The photo shows Ms. Stimpson's head
super-imposed onto a totally nude and outstretched model hired by
Heterodoxy editors David Horowitz and Peter Collier.  The caption
reads: "As their pin-up girl, as their centerfold, I'm not sure I
can judge (their impact)."
     The centerfold idea came about in response to comments Ms.
Stimpson made in the March 17, '93 Chronicle story about
Heterodoxy, in which Horowitz and Collier have more than once
poked fun at Stimpson.  "We wanted to fulfill her fantasy," said
editor Horowitz, who explained he thought that she might get a
kick out of it.  However, when Stimpson received a copy of the
issue via overnight mail along with a note saying, "Now you will
have to beat your admirers off with a stick," she was not amused.
In fact, she immediately handed the offensive material over to
her lawyer, Susan E. Holley, who labeled the photo "damaging and
malicious," and said it invaded Stimpson's privacy.
     Then Stimpson wrote an angry letter to the editor, calling
the photo "lascivious" and "demeaning." She demanded that they
remove it and apologize, none of which was done.
     Are Horowitz and Collier worried about possible legal
action?  Not in the least.  After all, noted Collier, a recent
issue of Spy maggie featured a front cover of Hillary Rodham
Clinton in full "S & M regalia."

