     Beware Of Truth: UCLA Econ Prof Trudy Cameron took a major
risk during the spring 1993 quarter of her Econ I class by
telling students with less than average high school GPAs to avoid
her class.  Students and faculty alike cringed when reading the
following in Cameron's course syllabus:
     "If you were admitted to UCLA with a GPA of less than 3.5
(as some students are to foster the worthwhile goal of
diversity), be aware that whatever is 'special' about you is
rarely apparent in a course of this size.  You will have to sink
or swim based on the objective criteria provided by exams,
quizzes and essay assignments.  Seek help early and often.  Hunt
up a tutor if you need one.  We will not compromise our grading
policy based on the identity of any student; grades will be based
solely on performance in this course."
     Reaction to these ideas was predictable.  Campus leftists
pounced on the syllabus wording, calling it a blatant attack on
"underrepresented students."  For example, when Prof. Brenda
Stevenson read the passage to her African-American History class,
one student reportedly pointed out that it was full of loaded
words and phrases that were potentially offensive.  However,
another student, freshman Yen Tran, viewed the syllabus not as
racist or biased but rather as direct and blatant.
     So why did Cameron choose to explain her course this way in
the first place?  "I felt compromised in the past," she said, by
students who suggested that she cut them some slack with the
grading because of their rough lives and minority status.  She
feels this helped to build momentum for what she called the
"federally sanctioned whining" that has become the norm on
college and university campuses."
     However, since pc is still in effect at UCLA, the offensive
passage was ultimately reworded to impart greater sensitivity to
beleaguered victim groups.

