I have a better idea.

Students ought to lobby the "accrediting" bodies for these schools to change
their regulations and add a couple of items for getting that lovely symbol of
excellance (or even just acceptance these days):

1)	Full transferrability of credits from any other accredited school at
	any undergraduate level.  
2)	Contractual language in their course program book (this has already
	been held to be a contract in several cases) which states that your
	academic record is <your property> at all times once tuition and 
	fees are paid.

This doesn't just mean credit hours -- it also means specialty credit, such
that if you have 60 hours in towards a BS in Computer Science and transfer,
you will still have 60 hours <and the commensurate class standing> that
would come with same.  

Further, it prevents "impounding" your academic record.

This means that you could then <painlessly> transfer up to your last year.

The point here is to enable you to vote with your wallet and feet, a concept
foreign to most educational institutions.  This would automatically put
abuses such as UH in immediate check -- they would then risk losing their
students, and thus enough of their funding base that they would collapse of
their own weight.

Those undertaking a post-graduate study level should insist on a contractual
document before signing up and matriculating for said course of study.  By
then I would expect that students would know enough of the world to both
insist on and hold colleges accountable to those contracts.

